<Base title="About Outrunning Ageing: who is asking, how the numbers are worked out, and what has moved them" description="Who built Outrunning Ageing and why, how the year at the top of the site is computed from the tree of treatments, and every paper read so far with what it settled or moved.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Who is asking, and how it works</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="section who" id="who">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Who is asking</p>
      <p>I'm John, and I write about AI, sentience and the spread of conscious life beyond Earth at <a href="https://sentient-horizons.com" rel="noopener">Sentient Horizons</a>. This is the fourth of a family of sites built the same way: <a href="https://beltequation.com" rel="noopener">The Belt Equation</a> tracks the odds of leaving Earth, <a href="https://thequietgalaxy.com" rel="noopener">The Quiet Galaxy</a> asks why the sky is quiet, and <a href="https://theupgradetree.com" rel="noopener">The Upgrade Tree</a> counts how much of a person can be changed on purpose today. Here the question is whether, and when, medicine starts giving back more than a year of healthy life for each year that passes. I wanted a place where that argument is laid out piece by piece, every number carries its source, and a new result moves the answer in public.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Why the name?</p>
      <p>Because the question is a race between two rates. Ageing takes about a year from a middle-aged person's remaining years every year, and medicine gives a little back. The site asks when the second overtakes the first, so that someone who keeps up with the best available care finds their remaining years holding steady or rising. Advocates of longevity science call that crossing <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longevity_escape_velocity" rel="noopener">longevity escape velocity</a>, a phrase coined in 2004. The site does not assume it is coming, and works out from the record how far off it looks.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

